hi this is press talker this map problem  went viral after it's dumped many  seventeen and eighteen year olds in  Australia can you figure it out a 50  cent coin has 12 sides of equal length  what is the angle theta formed when the  two coins are placed next to each other  on a table is it 12 degrees 30 degrees  36 degrees 60 degrees or 72 degrees give  this problem a try and when you're ready  keep watching the video for the solution  so there are many different ways to  solve this problem I'm going to go over  two ways that I thought about solving it  so we just had Halloween and one method  to solve this is to use a ghost coin  we'll remove the table and we'll make  another copy of the second coin now  let's translate this coin down and  you'll see that in the center is a  triangle formed by three of the sides of  the three coins so this triangle has all  of its sides equal to each other  and therefore it's an equilateral  triangle and each of its angles are 60  degrees therefore we can conclude the  answer is 60 degrees a more conventional  method is to consider exterior angles if  we extend the side between the two coins  that are touching the angle formed on  the outside is known as an exterior  angle in our diagram we have two  exterior angles and the angle we want  theta is equal to the sum of two  exterior angles now what's neat is we  can figure out what the value of the  exterior angle is in any polygon the  exterior angles always add up to 306  degrees you can think about this  intuitively because if you make one trip  around a polygon you always end up back  in the same spot and one trip around is  360 degrees so the measure of a single  exterior angle will be 360 divided by 12  because a 12 sided coin will have 12  exterior angles so a single exterior  angle is 30 degrees and that means 2  times an exterior angle or theta will be  60 degrees so we get to the same answer  did you figure it out thanks for  watching this video please subscribe to  my channel I make videos on math and  game theory you can catch me on my blog  mind your decisions which you can follow  on Facebook Google+ and patreon you can  also catch me on social media at presh  tal Walker and if you liked this video  please check out my books I write many  books about math puzzles and other  interesting math topics there are links  in the video description
 

